# Avoid TypeScript enums

Do not use `enum` or `const enum`. A closed set of values is a type-level problem. Enum is a TypeScript-only runtime object, so it is heavier than it looks and is not erasable syntax.

Use a string literal union when you only need the type. Use a `const` object when you also need runtime values. Use an `as const` array when the list itself matters.

```typescript
// ❌ TypeScript runtime construct, including const enum
enum OrderSide {
    Buy = 'buy',
    Sell = 'sell'
}

// ✅ union when the type is enough
type OrderSide = 'buy' | 'sell';

function isBuy(side: OrderSide): boolean {
    return side === 'buy';
}

// ✅ const object when you need runtime values
export const GridStatus = {
    Open: 'open',
    Flipped: 'flipped'
} as const;

export type GridStatus = (typeof GridStatus)[keyof typeof GridStatus];

// ✅ as const array when the list matters
export const gridSides = ['buy', 'sell'] as const;

export type GridSide = (typeof gridSides)[number];
```
